; ************************************************************************************************
; ************************************************************************************************
;
;		Name:		10object.divider
;		Purpose:	Marks the end of the runtime and the start of the compiler.
;		Created:	13th July 2026
;		Reviewed: 	No
;
; ************************************************************************************************
; ************************************************************************************************
;
;		Linked between the runtime components (runtime/common/ifloat32/polynomials) and
;		compiler.library, so ObjectBase is the first page the compiler occupies.
;
;		A saved OBJECT.PRG needs the runtime but has no use for the compiler, so
;		WriteObjectCode writes the object code out at ObjectBase rather than at FreeMemory.
;		That reclaims everything the compiler was using -- several KB in every compiled
;		program -- and lets the workspace start much lower, which is where the real win is.
;
;		NB the extension is deliberately not .asm: common-scripts/build.py globs every .asm
;		under source/ into _library.asm, so an .asm here would be assembled twice. Same
;		reason 00main.header and zzfree.footer are named as they are.
;
; ************************************************************************************************

		.section code

;
;		THE PADDING IS EMITTED EXPLICITLY, and it has to be. This used to be a bare ".align
;		256", which worked only because compiler.library was linked immediately after it: 64tass
;		emits alignment fill to place the bytes that FOLLOW, and trims the output at the last
;		byte written. With the compiler now in its own link there is nothing after this, so the
;		alignment produced no bytes at all and the image came out 78 bytes short of ObjectBase.
;
;		Those 78 bytes are never executed -- but a compiled program's p-code starts at
;		ObjectBase, so the runtime part of the file has to be exactly ObjectBase-$0801 long or
;		everything after it lands at the wrong address.
;
		.fill 	(256 - (* & $FF)) & $FF, $00 	; to the next page boundary -- WITH a fill
											; value, or 64tass reserves the space without
											; writing any bytes into the output file
ObjectBase:

		.send code
